Encryption

Byte layouts and derivations for packets addressed to a single or a
group destination. For what the layers are for, see upstream
docs/source/understanding.rst, section "Reaching the Destination".

Vectors: test/encrypted/, test/group/

See doc/packet for the header. Only the payload is described here.


Constants

    TOKEN_OVERHEAD          48 bytes   RNS/Cryptography/Token.py:50
    DERIVED_KEY_LENGTH      64 bytes   RNS/Identity.py:90
    PKCS7 blocksize         16 bytes   RNS/Cryptography/PKCS7.py:32


Payload

    offset  size  field
    0       32    ephemeral X25519 public key
    32      16    initialisation vector
    48      -     ciphertext, a multiple of 16 bytes
    -32     32    HMAC-SHA256

Everything from offset 32 onward is a token in the sense below. The
minimum payload is therefore 32 + 48 = 80 bytes, and the ciphertext is
never empty: PKCS7 adds a full block to a plaintext that is already
block aligned, and adds a whole block to an empty one.

    RNS/Identity.py:803


Key agreement

The sender draws a fresh X25519 key for every packet and transmits its
public half. The shared secret is

    shared = X25519(ephemeral_private, recipient_public)

where recipient_public is the X25519 half of the destination identity,
or a ratchet public key when one is known for that destination.

    RNS/Identity.py:804, RNS/Destination.py:607


Contributory behaviour, and the backend in the pin

X25519 against a point of small order yields an all-zero shared secret.
Neither end contributed to it, and every reader of the announce can
compute it, because the salt of the derivation is the recipient's
identity hash and that is public.

The two curve implementations python-rns ships do different things
with it.

    openssl backend     raises, produces no secret     the pin
    internal fallback   returns the 32 zero bytes      RNS/Cryptography/X25519.py

    RNS/Cryptography/Provider.py:35

Neither checks for it deliberately. RFC 7748 section 6.1 leaves the
choice to the protocol, and Reticulum makes none, so the behaviour is
whichever library is installed. See doc/identity for the same split on
the signature side, and for why the backend is part of the pin.

test/encrypted/low-order-ephemeral carries a packet whose ephemeral key
is the all-zero point. It decodes: the header is a header and the token
is laid out, because those bytes are in the packet. Everything that
needs the agreement is "-".

    shared_key         -
    signing_key        -
    encryption_key     -
    hmac_valid         -
    plaintext_length   -
    plaintext          -

That is a third value for hmac_valid beside yes and no, and it means
the question was never reached rather than answered. The same
distinction plaintext_length already carries.

An implementation that returns the zero secret rather than refusing
will report hmac_valid no here, not "-", and the vector will say so.


Key derivation

    derived = HKDF-SHA256(shared, salt, context, 64)

    salt      the recipient's identity hash, 16 bytes
    context   empty
    length    64 bytes

HKDF is RFC 5869 with SHA-256. The salt is the identity hash of the
recipient, derived from its own public key as in doc/identity. It stays
the identity hash even when the shared secret came from a ratchet: the
ratchet changes the agreement, not the salt.

    RNS/Identity.py:822, RNS/Identity.py:797, RNS/Cryptography/HKDF.py:35


Token

The 64 derived bytes are split, signing key first:

    offset  size  field
    0       32    HMAC key
    32      32    AES key

A 64-byte key selects AES-256-CBC. Token also accepts a 32-byte key,
which selects AES-128-CBC and halves both parts. Identity encryption
never produces one and neither does Token.generate_key, so the only way
to reach that mode is to configure a group destination with a 32-byte
key. No vector here covers it.

    RNS/Cryptography/Token.py:64

Verification comes before decryption. The HMAC covers the
initialisation vector and the ciphertext, not the ephemeral key:

    hmac = HMAC-SHA256(signing_key, iv || ciphertext)

A token whose HMAC does not match is rejected without decrypting.

    RNS/Cryptography/Token.py:77, RNS/Cryptography/Token.py:100

Reticulum calls this construction a token because it is Fernet with the
version byte and the eight-byte timestamp removed. An implementation
reaching for a Fernet library will produce nine bytes too many at the
front.

    RNS/Cryptography/Token.py:40


Padding

PKCS7 with a block size of 16. The final byte gives the padding length;
a value above the block size is invalid.

    RNS/Cryptography/PKCS7.py:34


Vector format

kind: encrypted, determinism: encode

raw is three lines:

    line 1    the recipient private key, 64 bytes
    line 2    the ratchet private key, 32 bytes, or "-"
    line 3    the complete packet

The private keys are in the vector because a packet cannot be decoded
without them. They are the upstream test keys and a ratchet generated
for the purpose; neither guards anything.

expect opens with the two keys, as recipient_private and
ratchet_private, so that it holds every byte of raw and the packet can
be rebuilt from it. It then carries the header fields, the payload
decomposition, and every intermediate value down to the plaintext:
shared_key, signing_key and encryption_key are all printed. An
implementation whose decryption fails can find the step where it
diverged rather than guessing.

    plaintext_length   the length in bytes, or "-" if nothing was
                       decrypted
    plaintext          the bytes as hex, or "-" when empty or absent

The two together are unambiguous: length 0 with plaintext "-" is an
empty plaintext, length "-" is a failure.

The three key fields and hmac_valid are "-" when no shared secret
exists at all. See the section on contributory behaviour above.

Encode class. The ephemeral key and the initialisation vector are drawn
at random, so no implementation can produce this packet a second time;
expect records both as they came out, so it can lay this one back out.
That is what cmd/check tests. It is also why the corpus carries no
vector that asks an implementation to encrypt: the reference ships a
token decryption vector and no token encryption vector for the same
reason.


Group destinations

Destination type 1. The payload is a bare token and nothing else:

    offset  size  field
    0       16    initialisation vector
    16      -     ciphertext, a multiple of 16 bytes
    -32     32    HMAC-SHA256

    RNS/Destination.py:612

There is no ephemeral key, because there is no key agreement, and no
HKDF, because there is nothing to derive from. The key is configuration
shared out of band, and the two halves of the token are the first and
second halves of it as configured.

    signing_key    = group_key[0:32]
    encryption_key = group_key[32:64]

That is the whole of what makes this type different, and it is a
difference in the direction an implementation is least likely to guess:
the same three fields, keyed by a value that went through no function.

An outbound group destination still needs an identity, and its hash is
derived exactly as a single destination's is. The identity plays no
part in the encryption.

    RNS/Destination.py:620

Vectors: test/group/minimal and test/group/altered-hmac.

kind: group, determinism: encode

raw is two lines:

    line 1    the group key, 64 bytes
    line 2    the complete packet

expect opens with the key, then the header fields, the three parts of
the token, the two halves of the key, and the plaintext. There is no
shared_key field: nothing was agreed.
